1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,352
Here is a request to port 3000,
where this app's frontend runs.

2
00:00:04,672 --> 00:00:07,125
It asks for two newsletters and gets them.

3
00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,282
But look who answered:

4
00:00:09,282 --> 00:00:14,293
uvicorn, the API's server,
and the API listens on port 9000.

5
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:17,093
Last year's version of this app
worked differently.

6
00:00:17,413 --> 00:00:23,542
Its page called port 9000 directly,
and every answer carried a permission slip:

7
00:00:23,542 --> 00:00:25,947
access-control-allow-origin: *.

8
00:00:26,183 --> 00:00:29,084
This year's API sends no such slip at all.

9
00:00:29,404 --> 00:00:32,615
So who carried the request
from 3000 to 9000,

10
00:00:32,615 --> 00:00:35,826
and why did last
year's page need permission?

11
00:00:36,067 --> 00:00:39,911
The lecture draws one chat message
as six numbered steps:

12
00:00:39,911 --> 00:00:44,835
request, forward, map into a container,
run a function, respond, show it.

13
00:00:45,155 --> 00:00:48,739
We add one step it never draws:
three and a half, the courier.

14
00:00:49,567 --> 00:00:53,251
Zoom out, and this is the lecture's
blueprint for the whole app,

15
00:00:53,251 --> 00:00:54,772
with the same three ports.

16
00:00:55,092 --> 00:00:57,729
The lecture calls the app ready,
then admits:

17
00:00:57,729 --> 00:00:59,956
well, not really, we need to build it.

18
00:01:00,276 --> 00:01:03,369
Today we build the right-hand side,
on your laptop.

19
00:01:03,617 --> 00:01:06,328
To follow along, clone both repositories,

20
00:01:06,328 --> 00:01:09,569
keep Docker running,
and free about 10 gigabytes.

21
00:01:09,889 --> 00:01:12,897
Your Google key matters only
for the chat answers.

22
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:16,250
The tracker in the corner
names the README step,

23
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:18,717
code panels name their file and lines,

24
00:01:18,717 --> 00:01:22,093
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

25
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:24,425
First, where this lecture sits.

26
00:01:24,745 --> 00:01:28,293
The course project
has its machine learning pipeline;

27
00:01:28,293 --> 00:01:32,510
what is still missing is the app:
a backend API and a frontend.

28
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:36,385
The lecture draws the system
as a technical architecture,

29
00:01:36,385 --> 00:01:38,617
the blueprint, one layer at a time.

30
00:01:38,937 --> 00:01:43,382
Developers, users, GitHub and Colab
sit outside the cloud,

31
00:01:43,382 --> 00:01:44,703
Vertex AI inside,

32
00:01:44,703 --> 00:01:48,899
and every arrow is labeled
with its protocol and port.

33
00:01:49,133 --> 00:01:53,080
Then come a bucket,
a registry of images and a persistent disk.

34
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:58,912
On the right sits a cluster:
NGINX in front, the app on 3000,

35
00:01:58,912 --> 00:02:02,978
the API on 9000,
the vector database on 8000.

36
00:02:03,216 --> 00:02:06,608
Today, that whole right-hand side
runs on your laptop.

37
00:02:07,166 --> 00:02:10,716
The lecture's folder rule:
one Git folder per app,

38
00:02:10,716 --> 00:02:15,614
one folder per service under src,
and secrets outside the Git folder.

39
00:02:15,934 --> 00:02:20,917
Version 1 even keeps empty folders
for the services it does not run;

40
00:02:20,917 --> 00:02:23,188
version 2 holds only its three.

41
00:02:23,433 --> 00:02:26,462
Steps 1 and 2: clone both repositories.

42
00:02:26,782 --> 00:02:30,686
The READMEs say clone or download;
git clone is our spelling.

43
00:02:31,006 --> 00:02:32,393
Pause and clone them.

44
00:02:32,633 --> 00:02:37,561
Step 3: next to the clones, create
secrets and persistent-folder yourself.

45
00:02:37,881 --> 00:02:40,798
Left to Docker on Linux,
they would belong to root,

46
00:02:40,798 --> 00:02:43,086
and the container could not write there.

47
00:02:43,406 --> 00:02:46,201
Your ml-workflow key goes into secrets.

48
00:02:46,450 --> 00:02:51,911
One thing changed on the day we recorded:
the main branch of version 2 moved twice.

49
00:02:52,231 --> 00:02:54,450
We follow main as you clone it today.

50
00:02:54,770 --> 00:03:00,197
The simple frontend the lecture teaches
now sits on a branch called 2025,

51
00:03:00,197 --> 00:03:02,130
which we visit at the end.

52
00:03:04,367 --> 00:03:07,972
A browser sends a request;
a server sends a response.

53
00:03:08,292 --> 00:03:10,811
A port picks which program gets it:

54
00:03:10,811 --> 00:03:15,202
public sites leave the default
80 and 443 out of the address;

55
00:03:15,202 --> 00:03:16,570
ours show the port.

56
00:03:16,817 --> 00:03:21,916
In this course, an API means a REST API,
built with FastAPI.

57
00:03:22,236 --> 00:03:27,334
Every client talks to that one API,
and only the API talks to the stores.

58
00:03:27,584 --> 00:03:29,739
Part 1, cheese-app-v1.

59
00:03:30,059 --> 00:03:35,883
Step 1: go to the API folder;
the README's path misses src, so this fails.

60
00:03:36,203 --> 00:03:38,314
A small slip: add src.

61
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,731
Step 2: sh docker-shell.sh.

62
00:03:42,051 --> 00:03:43,573
It builds the image,

63
00:03:43,573 --> 00:03:47,533
then starts a container
that removes itself on exit,

64
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:51,288
mounts your code,
and publishes port 9000 to 9000.

65
00:03:51,608 --> 00:03:52,760
Pause and run it.

66
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,816
Step 3: the README's long uvicorn line.

67
00:03:56,136 --> 00:04:00,936
Here, uvicorn is the server:
it owns the port and speaks HTTP.

68
00:04:01,256 --> 00:04:03,539
FastAPI is the app inside it.

69
00:04:03,784 --> 00:04:11,634
The phrase api.service:app is an address:
folder api, file service.py, variable app.

70
00:04:11,884 --> 00:04:17,505
--host 0.0.0.0 means every interface:
the container's own loopback,

71
00:04:17,505 --> 00:04:21,868
and its network interface,
where the tunnel arrives.

72
00:04:22,188 --> 00:04:25,932
--reload restarts the app
when a file in api changes;

73
00:04:25,932 --> 00:04:28,758
the last part passes on extra arguments.

74
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:37,960
The log agrees: 0.0.0.0, port 9000,
a reloader watching api, startup complete.

75
00:04:38,200 --> 00:04:41,312
In service.py, the app adds CORS middleware,

76
00:04:41,312 --> 00:04:46,264
which matters at the very end,
and one route: GET / returns a welcome.

77
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:49,401
Step 4: open localhost:9000.

78
00:04:49,721 --> 00:04:55,417
An extra curl shows the same reply:
status 200 from uvicorn, and the welcome.

79
00:04:55,737 --> 00:04:56,932
Pause and try it.

80
00:04:57,167 --> 00:05:00,820
That is the lecture's trip:
forwarded to the published port,

81
00:05:00,820 --> 00:05:05,082
mapped into the container,
the function runs, and the JSON comes back.

82
00:05:05,934 --> 00:05:11,929
One kind note: the slide labels
the inside of the container localhost:9000.

83
00:05:12,249 --> 00:05:19,950
Take it as port 9000 of the container:
uvicorn listens on 0.0.0.0.

84
00:05:20,184 --> 00:05:21,528
So, a quick test.

85
00:05:21,848 --> 00:05:29,677
If uvicorn listened on 127.0.0.1 instead,
would a curl from inside the container work?

86
00:05:29,997 --> 00:05:32,642
And your browser on localhost:9000?

87
00:05:42,583 --> 00:05:46,146
Inside, yes:
the container's own loopback answers.

88
00:05:46,466 --> 00:05:47,749
From the browser, no:

89
00:05:47,749 --> 00:05:51,842
the forwarded request arrives
on the container's network interface,

90
00:05:51,842 --> 00:05:53,186
where nothing listens.

91
00:05:53,506 --> 00:05:58,114
That is why the README says 0.0.0.0.

92
00:05:58,350 --> 00:06:02,702
Step 5: the lecture writes a route
as a decorated Python function.

93
00:06:03,022 --> 00:06:07,651
Add the README's square_root function
at the end of service.py, and save.

94
00:06:07,971 --> 00:06:11,342
It returns the square root
of x squared plus y squared.

95
00:06:11,583 --> 00:06:13,135
In the server's terminal,

96
00:06:13,135 --> 00:06:16,362
StatReload sees the change
and swaps in a fresh app.

97
00:06:16,682 --> 00:06:18,154
You restarted nothing.

98
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:21,593
Step 6: open square_root,
with its trailing slash.

99
00:06:21,913 --> 00:06:26,479
No arguments, so the defaults 1 and 2 apply:
the square root of 5.

100
00:06:26,799 --> 00:06:27,993
Pause and try it.

101
00:06:28,483 --> 00:06:31,854
Step 7: add x=2&y=10.

102
00:06:32,174 --> 00:06:35,520
The query string fills
the function's arguments,

103
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:38,168
and back comes the square root of 104.

104
00:06:38,416 --> 00:06:39,483
Two extras.

105
00:06:39,803 --> 00:06:44,923
A letter instead of a number
bounces off the float type hint with 422.

106
00:06:45,166 --> 00:06:47,317
Without the trailing slash,

107
00:06:47,317 --> 00:06:51,460
FastAPI answers 307 and points
to the slash version.

108
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:54,751
The server's log shows each request
with its status.

109
00:06:55,071 --> 00:07:00,126
200, 200, 200, 422, 307.

110
00:07:00,516 --> 00:07:02,585
Step 8: open /docs.

111
00:07:02,905 --> 00:07:07,511
FastAPI built this page from your code:
x and y appear as numbers,

112
00:07:07,511 --> 00:07:11,418
with their defaults,
and you can try a call right there.

113
00:07:11,666 --> 00:07:15,293
The type hints became the validation
and the documentation.

114
00:07:15,613 --> 00:07:18,301
Pause and try one call from the docs page.

115
00:07:19,650 --> 00:07:22,423
Now the other half of version 1: a page.

116
00:07:22,743 --> 00:07:26,673
The lecture splits a page
into HTML for structure,

117
00:07:26,673 --> 00:07:29,975
CSS for style and JavaScript for behavior.

118
00:07:30,295 --> 00:07:33,922
In version 1, index.html links all three.

119
00:07:34,167 --> 00:07:36,492
Step 1: go to the frontend folder.

120
00:07:36,812 --> 00:07:39,457
Same slip as before: add src.

121
00:07:39,700 --> 00:07:43,783
Steps 2 and 3:
pause, run sh docker-shell.sh,

122
00:07:43,783 --> 00:07:47,231
then http-server inside the container.

123
00:07:47,551 --> 00:07:51,348
It serves this folder on port 8080,
with a one-hour cache.

124
00:07:51,668 --> 00:07:55,143
The addresses it prints
are inside the container;

125
00:07:55,143 --> 00:07:56,703
your laptop uses 8080.

126
00:07:56,950 --> 00:07:59,745
Step 4: open localhost:8080.

127
00:08:00,065 --> 00:08:04,673
This is the lecture's first trip:
the request reaches http-server,

128
00:08:04,673 --> 00:08:07,745
a file comes back, and the browser draws it.

129
00:08:08,065 --> 00:08:09,387
Pause and open it.

130
00:08:16,883 --> 00:08:21,046
An extra look at main.js:
the podcasts are sample data,

131
00:08:21,046 --> 00:08:23,923
which would normally come from an API.

132
00:08:24,243 --> 00:08:26,227
Part 2 adds that second trip.

133
00:08:26,467 --> 00:08:29,944
Step 5: add a paragraph to index.html.

134
00:08:30,264 --> 00:08:33,837
A small note:
the README's snippet is an old whole body,

135
00:08:33,837 --> 00:08:37,283
and pasting it would drop the page
and its script tag.

136
00:08:37,603 --> 00:08:39,117
Add only the paragraph.

137
00:08:43,017 --> 00:08:46,004
Step 6: refresh, and the new text appears.

138
00:08:46,324 --> 00:08:49,625
Edits to main.js may need a hard refresh,

139
00:08:49,625 --> 00:08:54,537
because http-server lets
the browser cache files for an hour.

140
00:08:54,783 --> 00:08:58,794
To clean up, press Ctrl+C
and type exit in both terminals.

141
00:08:59,114 --> 00:09:03,210
The container list is then empty,
because both removed themselves.

142
00:09:05,583 --> 00:09:11,237
docker system prune asks first, so type y;
the image list shows what stays.

143
00:09:13,083 --> 00:09:17,883
Now version 2, the real app:
four containers on one Docker network.

144
00:09:18,203 --> 00:09:22,552
Chroma, the vector database, on 8000;
a loader;

145
00:09:22,552 --> 00:09:26,992
the API on 9000;
and the React frontend on 3000.

146
00:09:27,233 --> 00:09:28,677
The prerequisites:

147
00:09:28,677 --> 00:09:32,368
a Google Cloud project
with the Vertex AI API,

148
00:09:32,368 --> 00:09:36,292
the ml-workflow key
with the Vertex AI User role,

149
00:09:36,292 --> 00:09:38,134
and about 10 gigabytes.

150
00:09:38,454 --> 00:09:41,953
With your key,
chat and embedding calls bill your project.

151
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:43,303
One honest note:

152
00:09:43,303 --> 00:09:47,853
our recording's key file is well-formed
but belongs to no account.

153
00:09:48,173 --> 00:09:52,044
Everything that does not call
Google works exactly as for you;

154
00:09:52,044 --> 00:09:54,978
the first Google call fails,
and we show where.

155
00:09:55,216 --> 00:09:56,301
Last setup step:

156
00:09:56,301 --> 00:09:59,760
put your project ID
into both docker-shell scripts.

157
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:01,808
Ours shows a placeholder.

158
00:10:02,050 --> 00:10:05,869
The lecture's first step:
make sure the vector database is running.

159
00:10:06,189 --> 00:10:11,778
Step 1: cd src/vector-db,
and run docker-shell.sh.

160
00:10:12,098 --> 00:10:13,356
Pause and run them.

161
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:15,677
The script creates the network,

162
00:10:15,677 --> 00:10:18,691
builds the loader,
and Compose starts Chroma,

163
00:10:18,691 --> 00:10:22,922
publishing 8000 and keeping its data
in a folder on your laptop.

164
00:10:23,450 --> 00:10:27,546
Step 2, inside the container:
download and load the cheese books.

165
00:10:27,866 --> 00:10:34,010
Eleven files, one finished line per book,
14,356 chunks in all.

166
00:10:34,330 --> 00:10:35,866
Pause while yours loads.

167
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:39,342
These are the RAG
lecture's chunks and embeddings,

168
00:10:39,342 --> 00:10:41,092
downloaded, not recomputed.

169
00:10:41,450 --> 00:10:42,709
Step 3: chat.

170
00:10:43,029 --> 00:10:46,701
The first Google call,
embedding the question, fails here:

171
00:10:46,701 --> 00:10:48,789
Invalid grant: account not found.

172
00:10:49,109 --> 00:10:52,287
With your key,
an answer from the books appears instead.

173
00:10:52,533 --> 00:10:55,938
A small note:
chat always asks its own fixed question,

174
00:10:55,938 --> 00:10:59,658
"How is cheese made?",
and there is no place to type yours.

175
00:10:59,978 --> 00:11:03,434
To ask another, edit that line in cli.py.

176
00:11:03,683 --> 00:11:05,219
Step 4: exit.

177
00:11:05,539 --> 00:11:09,576
Chroma keeps running on 8000,
as an extra docker ps shows,

178
00:11:09,576 --> 00:11:14,030
and the network's phone book now
turns its name into an address.

179
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:17,815
Backend APIs.

180
00:11:18,135 --> 00:11:22,720
In a new terminal,
from the cheese-app-v2 folder:

181
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:27,586
step 1, cd src/api-service,
and run docker-shell.sh.

182
00:11:27,906 --> 00:11:29,672
The first build takes a while,

183
00:11:29,672 --> 00:11:32,322
because of TensorFlow,
so pause and start it.

184
00:11:32,567 --> 00:11:36,535
The banner offers two commands:
api_server and pytest.

185
00:11:36,783 --> 00:11:38,307
This script checks first:

186
00:11:38,307 --> 00:11:41,903
if the container already runs,
it just opens a shell in it.

187
00:11:42,223 --> 00:11:45,572
Otherwise it mounts secrets
without write access,

188
00:11:45,572 --> 00:11:48,761
mounts your persistent-folder,
publishes 9000,

189
00:11:48,761 --> 00:11:50,287
and joins the network.

190
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,827
Step 2: api_server.

191
00:11:53,147 --> 00:11:59,227
It is almost the same uvicorn line
as before, pointing at api.service:app.

192
00:11:59,547 --> 00:12:00,805
Pause and start it.

193
00:12:01,050 --> 00:12:03,930
Application startup complete
arrives at once.

194
00:12:04,250 --> 00:12:06,984
Only after it do TensorFlow's lines appear,

195
00:12:06,984 --> 00:12:10,671
from a background thread
that loads the cheese classifier.

196
00:12:10,917 --> 00:12:12,210
That is the design:

197
00:12:12,210 --> 00:12:15,546
the API answers right away
while the model loads.

198
00:12:15,866 --> 00:12:19,770
Two probes say how far along it is:
/healthz and /readyz.

199
00:12:21,133 --> 00:12:25,208
Step 3: /healthz says 200:
the process is alive.

200
00:12:25,528 --> 00:12:29,069
/readyz says 503, CNN model is loading.

201
00:12:34,250 --> 00:12:38,920
An extra look: the model is downloaded once
into your persistent-folder,

202
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:40,607
and every start reuses it.

203
00:12:41,350 --> 00:12:44,123
Another extra: the version 1 habits fail.

204
00:12:44,443 --> 00:12:46,392
/ and /docs answer 404;

205
00:12:46,392 --> 00:12:53,254
everything the browser calls is under /api,
and only the probes stay at the root.

206
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:03,844
So, a quick test.

207
00:13:04,164 --> 00:13:07,791
/healthz says 200 and /readyz says 503.

208
00:13:08,111 --> 00:13:10,265
Do you restart the container, or wait?

209
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:22,747
Wait: about nine seconds later,
/readyz says 200:

210
00:13:22,747 --> 00:13:25,649
the model is loaded and Chroma answers.

211
00:13:25,969 --> 00:13:28,529
A restart would only start the loading over.

212
00:13:28,849 --> 00:13:32,496
Kubernetes,
which you meet in lectures 19 and 22,

213
00:13:32,496 --> 00:13:34,951
can use exactly these two probes:

214
00:13:34,951 --> 00:13:38,407
restart on liveness,
send traffic on readiness.

215
00:13:39,650 --> 00:13:41,677
Which routes does the API need?

216
00:13:41,997 --> 00:13:44,249
The lecture starts from the screens:

217
00:13:44,249 --> 00:13:47,565
newsletters, podcasts,
and a chat that takes a photo.

218
00:13:47,885 --> 00:13:49,267
Each screen needs data,

219
00:13:49,267 --> 00:13:52,151
and each kind of data
becomes a group of routes.

220
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:58,181
In service.py, newsletters and podcasts
get a router each under /api.

221
00:13:58,501 --> 00:14:01,859
Then one function
stamps out three chat routers:

222
00:14:01,859 --> 00:14:04,027
Ask, Identify and Cheese books.

223
00:14:04,347 --> 00:14:09,339
The lecture shows six groups;
agent chat has no router in this repository.

224
00:14:09,583 --> 00:14:15,684
The lecture also shows three places an agent
can sit, with or without an MCP service.

225
00:14:16,004 --> 00:14:17,733
This repository has none.

226
00:14:17,967 --> 00:14:20,719
And the slide's one-file route is the idea.

227
00:14:21,039 --> 00:14:27,076
The repository puts the same function
in a router, mounted at /api/newsletters.

228
00:14:27,317 --> 00:14:31,413
Demo route: add the README's function
after get_status, and save.

229
00:14:31,733 --> 00:14:33,631
The server reloads by itself.

230
00:14:35,050 --> 00:14:39,914
This loads the model again,
and /readyz is back to 200 shortly after.

231
00:14:40,150 --> 00:14:44,481
Then open /api/square_root:
the square root of 5 again.

232
00:14:44,801 --> 00:14:49,387
With x=3&y=4, it answers 5.0.

233
00:14:49,707 --> 00:14:51,073
Pause and try both.

234
00:14:51,317 --> 00:14:54,901
API docs: they moved to /api/docs.

235
00:14:55,221 --> 00:15:00,106
An extra listing shows all 25 operations:
the leaves of this tree.

236
00:15:00,883 --> 00:15:04,531
In the docs, pause and try
get newsletters with a limit of 2.

237
00:15:04,851 --> 00:15:08,192
Two newsletters come back as JSON,
newest first:

238
00:15:08,192 --> 00:15:10,419
the lecture's example, now real.

239
00:15:10,739 --> 00:15:14,323
An unknown ID gets 404,
newsletter not found.

240
00:15:14,567 --> 00:15:17,767
Review the code:
the README's table maps each file.

241
00:15:18,087 --> 00:15:22,236
service.py builds the app,
settings.py holds every setting,

242
00:15:22,236 --> 00:15:24,231
the routers hold the routes,

243
00:15:24,231 --> 00:15:27,580
and one utils file per chat
mode does the work.

244
00:15:28,117 --> 00:15:32,192
The lecture's chat handler says:
use Gemini to generate text.

245
00:15:32,512 --> 00:15:36,906
In the repository, one route starts a chat
and another continues it.

246
00:15:37,226 --> 00:15:41,749
The real path names the mode:
/api/llm/chats.

247
00:15:41,984 --> 00:15:44,864
How does the API remember a conversation?

248
00:15:45,184 --> 00:15:47,573
It does not: with every message,

249
00:15:47,573 --> 00:15:52,053
the browser sends a session ID,
one random UUID per browser.

250
00:15:52,373 --> 00:15:55,134
The API opens that session's chat file,

251
00:15:55,134 --> 00:15:58,816
adds your message and the reply, saves,
and forgets.

252
00:15:59,050 --> 00:16:04,448
Two extras: no session ID,
or an empty message, gets 422.

253
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:16,331
Identify's photo step works fully offline.

254
00:16:16,651 --> 00:16:20,230
We send the repository's
own blue cheese photo,

255
00:16:20,230 --> 00:16:23,733
and the CNN guesses parmigiano,
at 58 percent.

256
00:16:24,053 --> 00:16:26,720
A follow-up question would go to Gemini.

257
00:16:26,967 --> 00:16:29,320
An extra listing shows it on disk:

258
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:35,202
one JSON file per chat under your session,
and the photo in the mode's images folder.

259
00:16:35,484 --> 00:16:41,287
Ask mode calls Gemini, and here it stops:
Internal Server Error, status 500.

260
00:16:41,607 --> 00:16:46,705
The API's terminal shows why:
the same Invalid grant: account not found.

261
00:16:47,025 --> 00:16:49,798
With your key,
Gemini's answer has this shape.

262
00:16:50,034 --> 00:16:57,415
A small note: the README's table covers
Gemini 403 and 404, reported as 502.

263
00:16:57,735 --> 00:17:01,332
A key Google cannot sign
in with fails earlier,

264
00:17:01,332 --> 00:17:05,159
as a plain 500,
so check the API's terminal first.

265
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:06,872
Two more README notes.

266
00:17:07,192 --> 00:17:11,252
Each message rebuilds the conversation
from the saved file,

267
00:17:11,252 --> 00:17:13,592
so any copy of the API can answer.

268
00:17:13,912 --> 00:17:16,945
And routes written
with plain def run in threads,

269
00:17:16,945 --> 00:17:19,608
so one slow answer does not block the rest.

270
00:17:19,850 --> 00:17:24,082
Last, the tests:
in a second terminal, in the same folder,

271
00:17:24,082 --> 00:17:28,533
docker-shell.sh again opens a shell
in the running container.

272
00:17:28,853 --> 00:17:31,221
Run pytest: 26 passed.

273
00:17:31,541 --> 00:17:32,693
Pause and run it.

274
00:17:33,167 --> 00:17:34,810
Keep this container running.

275
00:17:35,130 --> 00:17:39,183
The API is ready,
but nobody can use it from a browser yet.

276
00:17:39,503 --> 00:17:44,922
In part 2 the browser talks to port 3000,
so how does that reach 9000?

277
00:17:46,150 --> 00:17:47,814
Part 2: the frontend.

278
00:17:48,134 --> 00:17:52,635
The API is up on port 9000;
now a page that uses it.

279
00:17:52,883 --> 00:17:56,147
The lecture starts
with one HTML page per feature.

280
00:17:56,467 --> 00:17:59,430
Each carries its own header,
script and style;

281
00:17:59,430 --> 00:18:03,294
nothing is shared,
and every click loads a whole page: slow.

282
00:18:03,667 --> 00:18:07,784
The fix is a single-page app built
from components, with a framework.

283
00:18:08,104 --> 00:18:11,112
The lecture names the common
ones and picks React.

284
00:18:11,350 --> 00:18:16,513
In React, a component is a function
that returns markup, written in JSX.

285
00:18:16,833 --> 00:18:20,803
The app is one shell:
a header defined only once, a footer,

286
00:18:20,803 --> 00:18:24,235
and in between a block
that changes with each page.

287
00:18:24,483 --> 00:18:27,304
Today's repository uses React with Vite,

288
00:18:27,304 --> 00:18:31,182
a development server and build tool,
plus React Router.

289
00:18:31,502 --> 00:18:35,547
In main.jsx,
a route table maps each address to a page,

290
00:18:35,547 --> 00:18:38,563
with a star that catches everything else.

291
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:42,115
In App.jsx,
the header and footer are written once,

292
00:18:42,115 --> 00:18:47,056
and between them sits the outlet,
the window where the current page appears.

293
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:52,730
React setup, in a new terminal
from the cheese-app-v2 folder:

294
00:18:52,730 --> 00:18:57,625
step 1, cd src/frontend-react,
and run docker-shell.sh.

295
00:18:57,945 --> 00:19:02,645
It mounts your code,
keeps node_modules in a named volume,

296
00:19:02,645 --> 00:19:06,777
publishes 3000 to port 5173,
and joins the network.

297
00:19:07,097 --> 00:19:08,249
Pause and run it.

298
00:19:08,483 --> 00:19:10,680
The banner shows Node and npm.

299
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:14,877
Your first run installs the dependencies
into the volume;

300
00:19:14,877 --> 00:19:16,782
ours was already up to date.

301
00:19:17,017 --> 00:19:19,214
Step 2: npm run dev.

302
00:19:19,534 --> 00:19:23,554
Vite is ready
and prints two addresses on 5173;

303
00:19:23,554 --> 00:19:28,601
both are inside the container,
so on your laptop, use 3000.

304
00:19:28,921 --> 00:19:30,179
Pause and start it.

305
00:19:30,417 --> 00:19:33,212
Step 3: open localhost:3000.

306
00:19:33,532 --> 00:19:39,804
The HTML is almost empty:
one div called root and a script, main.jsx.

307
00:19:40,124 --> 00:19:43,110
The whole app is one page
and its JavaScript.

308
00:19:43,350 --> 00:19:49,515
An extra check: every address, even one
that does not exist, returns the same HTML.

309
00:19:49,835 --> 00:19:52,417
The browser picks the page, not the server.

310
00:19:55,383 --> 00:19:56,770
Now the hook's answer.

311
00:19:57,090 --> 00:20:00,631
In the lecture,
the page's JavaScript calls the API.

312
00:20:00,951 --> 00:20:07,174
Here, every call in api.js goes
to a relative path starting with /api,

313
00:20:07,174 --> 00:20:10,551
with the session ID from localStorage.

314
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:14,295
And vite.config.js holds the courier:

315
00:20:14,295 --> 00:20:20,812
a proxy rule that forwards /api
to cheese-app-api-service, port 9000,

316
00:20:20,812 --> 00:20:22,512
by container name.

317
00:20:22,983 --> 00:20:26,546
So the browser only ever
talks to localhost:3000.

318
00:20:26,866 --> 00:20:29,198
Vite, in the frontend container,

319
00:20:29,198 --> 00:20:32,476
carries each /api request
across the network.

320
00:20:32,796 --> 00:20:35,698
That is why uvicorn answered on 3000.

321
00:20:35,933 --> 00:20:38,898
An origin is the scheme,
the host and the port.

322
00:20:39,218 --> 00:20:42,458
The page and its API calls share one origin,

323
00:20:42,458 --> 00:20:48,200
so the browser has nothing to object to,
and the API needs no permission slip.

324
00:20:48,450 --> 00:20:49,794
So, a quick test.

325
00:20:50,114 --> 00:20:56,215
Could the page's JavaScript call the API
by its container name, port 9000, directly?

326
00:21:05,050 --> 00:21:09,664
No. The JavaScript runs in your browser,
on the laptop,

327
00:21:09,664 --> 00:21:12,901
and the laptop's DNS
knows no container names.

328
00:21:13,221 --> 00:21:19,130
Only code inside the network can use them:
the Vite proxy, nginx, the API.

329
00:21:19,366 --> 00:21:20,810
An extra experiment:

330
00:21:20,810 --> 00:21:24,273
unplug the API from the network
for one request.

331
00:21:24,593 --> 00:21:27,515
The browser gets 502 with an empty body:

332
00:21:27,515 --> 00:21:31,825
the README's troubleshooting row
for pages that load empty.

333
00:21:32,066 --> 00:21:33,095
Review the app:

334
00:21:33,095 --> 00:21:36,660
home, podcasts and newsletters load
through the API,

335
00:21:36,660 --> 00:21:40,173
and the Recent tab keeps your chats
across a reload.

336
00:21:40,493 --> 00:21:43,447
In chat, Identify works through the proxy;

337
00:21:43,447 --> 00:21:48,087
Ask, and Cheese books
with its Tolminc question, get the same 500.

338
00:21:48,407 --> 00:21:49,752
Pause and click through.

339
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:53,226
The README's code table
names the files we just saw;

340
00:21:53,226 --> 00:21:54,715
pause to match each one.

341
00:21:55,035 --> 00:21:58,424
Then, in a second terminal
in the same folder,

342
00:21:58,424 --> 00:22:02,918
docker-shell.sh again opens a shell
in the running container;

343
00:22:02,918 --> 00:22:04,265
run npm run check:

344
00:22:04,265 --> 00:22:07,707
a lint,
a format check and a production build.

345
00:22:08,027 --> 00:22:10,160
That build is what production serves.

346
00:22:11,400 --> 00:22:17,011
In production, Vite does not run,
so who serves the files and forwards /api?

347
00:22:17,331 --> 00:22:18,632
Nginx does.

348
00:22:18,952 --> 00:22:22,532
Today's README dropped this section
on the recording day,

349
00:22:22,532 --> 00:22:24,566
but its files are still in main,

350
00:22:24,566 --> 00:22:27,997
so we run the earlier README's commands,
marked extra.

351
00:22:28,233 --> 00:22:30,836
The production Dockerfile has two stages.

352
00:22:31,156 --> 00:22:33,054
The first builds the app with Node.

353
00:22:33,374 --> 00:22:37,577
The second starts from nginx
and copies in only the built files.

354
00:22:37,817 --> 00:22:41,758
The nginx template
has the same rule as Vite:

355
00:22:41,758 --> 00:22:44,473
/api goes to the API container.

356
00:22:44,793 --> 00:22:50,731
Any other page address gets index.html,
so the router can choose the page;

357
00:22:50,731 --> 00:22:53,539
only a missing asset is a real 404.

358
00:22:53,783 --> 00:22:56,588
Stop the dev server with Ctrl+C and exit,

359
00:22:56,588 --> 00:22:59,735
then build the production image,
tagged :prod.

360
00:23:00,055 --> 00:23:03,426
Its first stage runs the same
Vite build you just checked.

361
00:23:03,667 --> 00:23:07,422
Run it on the network,
publishing 8080 to port 80.

362
00:23:07,742 --> 00:23:11,767
Open localhost:8080:
the server is now nginx,

363
00:23:11,767 --> 00:23:17,491
/api still reaches the API,
and a deep link still gets the page.

364
00:23:17,733 --> 00:23:19,371
Compare the sizes:

365
00:23:19,371 --> 00:23:24,560
331 megabytes for the dev image,
about 95 for production.

366
00:23:24,880 --> 00:23:28,400
No Node, no Vite:
just files and one proxy rule.

367
00:23:28,650 --> 00:23:32,426
The lecture's production slide:
only the outside changes.

368
00:23:32,746 --> 00:23:37,850
In lecture 20, NGINX goes in front
on a cloud machine, on port 80;

369
00:23:37,850 --> 00:23:40,789
the API's code and port stay the same.

370
00:23:41,033 --> 00:23:46,686
As an extra, stop it, stop the API too,
and start the production container again.

371
00:23:47,006 --> 00:23:48,244
It will not start.

372
00:23:48,564 --> 00:23:49,689
So, a quick test:

373
00:23:49,689 --> 00:23:53,065
why would a page server care
whether the API is up?

374
00:23:59,900 --> 00:24:02,993
It exits at once:
host not found in upstream.

375
00:24:03,313 --> 00:24:08,476
Nginx looks up the proxy target's name
when it starts, and the API is gone.

376
00:24:08,796 --> 00:24:10,481
Start the API first.

377
00:24:11,717 --> 00:24:14,106
Instead of three terminals, one command.

378
00:24:14,426 --> 00:24:18,778
Step 1: put your project ID
into the compose file in src.

379
00:24:19,098 --> 00:24:20,336
It appears twice.

380
00:24:20,584 --> 00:24:23,016
The file names the same four services.

381
00:24:23,336 --> 00:24:26,921
The loader first checks
whether the books are already in Chroma,

382
00:24:26,921 --> 00:24:28,434
and loads them only if not.

383
00:24:28,684 --> 00:24:34,849
Step 2: stop whatever still runs,
then docker compose down in src/vector-db.

384
00:24:35,169 --> 00:24:37,796
Compose warns about two unset variables;

385
00:24:37,796 --> 00:24:40,950
the shell scripts set them,
so this is harmless.

386
00:24:41,200 --> 00:24:45,467
Step 3: change into src,
and docker compose up --build.

387
00:24:45,787 --> 00:24:46,939
Pause and run it.

388
00:24:47,534 --> 00:24:50,866
Two warnings:
the network and the volume already exist,

389
00:24:50,866 --> 00:24:52,441
made by the shell scripts.

390
00:24:52,761 --> 00:24:54,681
They are warnings, not errors.

391
00:24:55,001 --> 00:24:56,963
Then four containers are created.

392
00:24:58,667 --> 00:25:03,254
Chroma, uvicorn and Vite start together,
and the loader exits with 0:

393
00:25:03,254 --> 00:25:05,515
it found the books already loaded.

394
00:25:09,984 --> 00:25:14,485
Step 4:
open localhost:3000 and the API docs.

395
00:25:14,805 --> 00:25:19,456
A small slip:
the README prints port 900; use 9000.

396
00:25:19,700 --> 00:25:25,140
Step 5: Ctrl+C, then docker compose down
removes all four containers.

397
00:25:26,117 --> 00:25:27,210
Docker cleanup:

398
00:25:27,210 --> 00:25:30,490
compose down
in vector-db finds nothing left.

399
00:25:30,810 --> 00:25:32,695
Docker system prune asks first,

400
00:25:32,695 --> 00:25:34,174
then deletes the network

401
00:25:34,174 --> 00:25:37,317
and about four and a half
gigabytes of build cache.

402
00:25:37,567 --> 00:25:39,772
Prune keeps every tagged image,

403
00:25:39,772 --> 00:25:43,045
including the API's image
of over 4 gigabytes,

404
00:25:43,045 --> 00:25:45,012
and the node_modules volume.

405
00:25:45,332 --> 00:25:47,850
Remove those by name if you need the disk.

406
00:25:49,083 --> 00:25:52,710
Now Tutorial 24,
the lecture's simple frontend.

407
00:25:53,030 --> 00:25:55,927
Main dropped it on the recording day;

408
00:25:55,927 --> 00:26:00,624
branch 2025 keeps it,
with last year's API, which serves it.

409
00:26:00,944 --> 00:26:04,141
Branch 2025 builds its own big images:

410
00:26:04,141 --> 00:26:08,347
if your disk is tight,
remove main's images first.

411
00:26:08,583 --> 00:26:11,150
git switch 2025 refuses at first:

412
00:26:11,150 --> 00:26:14,727
your edits to four files
would be overwritten.

413
00:26:15,047 --> 00:26:17,116
An extra stash puts them aside.

414
00:26:18,333 --> 00:26:22,088
An extra log shows last year's commit,
with frontend-simple.

415
00:26:22,408 --> 00:26:25,495
Its vector-db script
wants a second key name,

416
00:26:25,495 --> 00:26:29,405
so copy your key under it,
and set your project ID again.

417
00:26:32,583 --> 00:26:38,733
Vector DB steps 1 to 3,
from the folder above cheese-app-v2,

418
00:26:38,733 --> 00:26:45,703
as the 2025 README spells them,
end in the same load: 14,356 chunks.

419
00:26:46,023 --> 00:26:49,095
Its Chroma image is tagged latest
and still works.

420
00:26:49,415 --> 00:26:51,826
Leave with exit; Chroma keeps running.

421
00:26:52,383 --> 00:26:58,946
Backend steps 1, 2 and 4, in a new terminal
from the folder that holds both clones:

422
00:26:58,946 --> 00:27:04,244
change into the API folder,
sh docker-shell.sh, and uvicorn_server.

423
00:27:04,564 --> 00:27:05,823
Pause and start it.

424
00:27:06,483 --> 00:27:09,947
Two differences from main:
the reloader is WatchFiles,

425
00:27:09,947 --> 00:27:13,538
and the model loads
before application startup complete,

426
00:27:13,538 --> 00:27:16,296
so start-up waits, with no readiness probe.

427
00:27:18,016 --> 00:27:22,837
Then verify localhost:9000:
welcome to AC215.

428
00:27:23,157 --> 00:27:28,527
A small note: the demo route
and the routers the 2025 README asks you

429
00:27:28,527 --> 00:27:32,885
to enable are already in the code,
except RAG and agent.

430
00:27:33,205 --> 00:27:36,235
Don't paste them again,
or the route is duplicated.

431
00:27:36,483 --> 00:27:40,761
Also, last year's Gemini model is retired:
even with a key,

432
00:27:40,761 --> 00:27:44,726
its chat fails until the model
and region are updated;

433
00:27:44,726 --> 00:27:46,488
main uses a current one.

434
00:27:47,450 --> 00:27:51,663
And what matters next:
CORS middleware that allows every origin,

435
00:27:51,663 --> 00:27:54,362
the permission slip main no longer needs.

436
00:27:55,683 --> 00:28:01,891
Tutorial 24, step 1:
cd cheese-app-v2/src/frontend-simple.

437
00:28:02,211 --> 00:28:08,355
Steps 2 and 3: sh docker-shell.sh,
then http-server on 8080.

438
00:28:08,675 --> 00:28:09,934
Pause and start it.

439
00:28:10,183 --> 00:28:13,063
Home page: open index.html.

440
00:28:13,383 --> 00:28:17,522
The landing page links to two more pages:
newsletters and chat.

441
00:28:17,766 --> 00:28:21,371
Newsletter feature: open newsletters.html.

442
00:28:21,691 --> 00:28:25,382
Its script names the API: localhost:9000.

443
00:28:25,702 --> 00:28:28,294
This is the lecture's second trip:

444
00:28:28,294 --> 00:28:33,020
the page came from 8080,
and its JavaScript calls 9000 itself.

445
00:28:33,266 --> 00:28:36,402
Same laptop, different port,
so a different origin.

446
00:28:36,722 --> 00:28:41,032
Now the browser checks every answer:
did the server allow this origin?

447
00:28:41,266 --> 00:28:44,125
An extra curl sends what the browser sends.

448
00:28:44,445 --> 00:28:48,393
The API answers
with access-control-allow-origin *,

449
00:28:48,393 --> 00:28:49,864
permission granted.

450
00:28:52,183 --> 00:28:54,388
First comes 307, then 200:

451
00:28:54,388 --> 00:28:58,711
the paths differ by one slash,
an extra round trip.

452
00:28:58,950 --> 00:29:03,835
Chat feature:
open chat.html and ask how cheese is made.

453
00:29:04,155 --> 00:29:07,570
Now the browser adds a step
the lecture never draws:

454
00:29:07,570 --> 00:29:09,211
step zero, the preflight.

455
00:29:11,433 --> 00:29:15,444
A JSON post with a custom header
makes the browser ask first,

456
00:29:15,444 --> 00:29:17,022
with an OPTIONS request.

457
00:29:17,342 --> 00:29:23,145
The answer allows any origin, these methods
and these two headers, for 600 seconds.

458
00:29:23,866 --> 00:29:30,385
The log: OPTIONS 200, then the post
bounces with 307 and ends in 500,

459
00:29:30,385 --> 00:29:32,463
the same Gemini error.

460
00:29:32,783 --> 00:29:37,797
That 500 carries the permission header too,
so the browser shows the real error.

461
00:29:38,033 --> 00:29:41,834
So CORS is the browser's rule
about the server's permission;

462
00:29:41,834 --> 00:29:44,177
curl and the docs page never meet it.

463
00:29:44,416 --> 00:29:45,760
So, a quick test.

464
00:29:46,080 --> 00:29:50,261
Last year's page needed CORS,
and this year's React app does not.

465
00:29:50,581 --> 00:29:54,614
What changed: the API, the browser,
or the origin it calls?

466
00:30:03,450 --> 00:30:04,453
The origin.

467
00:30:04,773 --> 00:30:09,978
The React page calls its own origin,
and a proxy forwards: nothing to permit.

468
00:30:10,298 --> 00:30:14,010
The API then dropped the middleware,
because nothing needed it.

469
00:30:14,250 --> 00:30:17,493
To stop, Ctrl+C and exit in both terminals.

470
00:30:17,813 --> 00:30:20,258
As extras, stop last year's Chroma,

471
00:30:20,258 --> 00:30:23,471
discard the branch's edits
and switch to main;

472
00:30:23,471 --> 00:30:25,471
git stash pop restores yours.

473
00:30:26,717 --> 00:30:30,344
Before you go, a cleanup checklist,
all done once already.

474
00:30:30,664 --> 00:30:34,182
Exit every container,
compose down, back to main,

475
00:30:34,182 --> 00:30:36,336
prune after checking its list;

476
00:30:36,336 --> 00:30:40,520
then remove the node_modules
volume and big images by name.

477
00:30:40,840 --> 00:30:42,589
Your key stays in secrets.

478
00:30:42,834 --> 00:30:46,022
Here is the whole trip,
with the hook answered:

479
00:30:46,022 --> 00:30:48,125
the browser talks only to 3000.

480
00:30:48,445 --> 00:30:50,660
Inside, Vite in development

481
00:30:50,660 --> 00:30:55,828
or nginx in production carries /api
by name to uvicorn on 9000,

482
00:30:55,828 --> 00:30:58,535
where a router runs the function.

483
00:30:58,855 --> 00:31:03,570
The JSON comes back the same way:
one origin, no permission needed.

484
00:31:04,234 --> 00:31:05,983
Back to the lecture's blueprint.

485
00:31:06,303 --> 00:31:08,885
Your four boxes rise into the cluster.

486
00:31:09,205 --> 00:31:13,760
NGINX in front, the registry
and the persistent disk are still dashed:

487
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:15,647
the next lectures build them.

488
00:31:15,884 --> 00:31:16,977
For your project,

489
00:31:16,977 --> 00:31:21,477
the lecture asks you to draw
your solution and technical architecture,

490
00:31:21,477 --> 00:31:25,014
and points to a crash course
and a template repository.

491
00:31:25,250 --> 00:31:27,191
Test yourself, pausing on each.

492
00:31:27,511 --> 00:31:30,157
One: trace a newsletters request on main.

493
00:31:30,477 --> 00:31:35,213
Which container does the browser talk to,
and where does port 9000 come in?

494
00:31:35,450 --> 00:31:42,287
Two: what do api.service:app,
--host 0.0.0.0 and --reload each do,

495
00:31:42,287 --> 00:31:47,674
and why does square_root
without a slash answer 307?

496
00:31:47,917 --> 00:31:49,880
Three: /healthz and /readyz.

497
00:31:50,200 --> 00:31:52,269
What does each one check in this API?

498
00:31:52,517 --> 00:31:55,751
Four:
where does a chat stay between two messages,

499
00:31:55,751 --> 00:31:58,469
and why can any copy of the API answer it?

500
00:31:58,717 --> 00:32:00,545
Five: which file decides

501
00:32:00,545 --> 00:32:04,508
where /api goes in development,
which in production,

502
00:32:04,508 --> 00:32:09,021
and why did last year's API need CORS
while main's does not?

